Treat `sys.platform == "android"` as Linux so psutil builds and imports there.

psutil chooses its platform backend from a block of constants at the top of
psutil/_common.py, and `LINUX` is the gate for the `_psutil_linux` /
`_psutil_posix` extensions. It tests `sys.platform.startswith("linux")`.
CPython on Android reports "android" (a distinct value since 3.13), so that
gate is False -- and so is every other platform flag in the block -- and
psutil concludes it is running somewhere it does not support.

Because _common.py is shared by the build and the runtime, that lands twice:

  * setup.py does `from _common import LINUX` and ends its platform chain
    with `sys.exit("platform {} is not supported")`, so the wheel never
    builds;
  * psutil/__init__.py runs the same chain at import and raises
    `NotImplementedError: platform android is not supported`, so even a wheel
    smuggled onto a device would fail on `import psutil`.

Android is a Linux kernel with /proc, which is exactly what `_psutil_linux`
reads, so the right answer is to let "android" satisfy the Linux gate:
`startswith(("linux", "android"))`. One line, and it covers both halves --
the correct extension is selected at build time and the same constant answers
correctly at import.

The rest of psutil needs no adjustment; this is a naming gap, not a porting
job. Note also that the recipe is Android-only (`package.platforms:
[android]`): on iOS the equivalent gate would select the macOS backend
`_psutil_osx`, which links IOKit and calls libproc / host_processor_info /
macOS-only sysctls that iOS either does not expose or restricts inside the
app sandbox, so no analogous one-liner would help there.

--- a/psutil/_common.py	2025-11-26 18:30:32
+++ b/psutil/_common.py	2026-06-15 17:28:13
@@ -71,7 +71,7 @@
 
 POSIX = os.name == "posix"
 WINDOWS = os.name == "nt"
-LINUX = sys.platform.startswith("linux")
+LINUX = sys.platform.startswith(("linux", "android"))
 MACOS = sys.platform.startswith("darwin")
 OSX = MACOS  # deprecated alias
 FREEBSD = sys.platform.startswith(("freebsd", "midnightbsd"))
